        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A portable power station runs a refrigerator without difficulty, provided the inverter can absorb the compressor's startup surge. Check for a 1,000W continuous rating with roughly 2,000W of surge headroom and the output side of the question is settled.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Runtime comes down to duty cycle, and that is what makes the numbers better than people expect. A modern fridge consumes one to two kilowatt-hours a day rather than the three or four a naive calculation predicts, so 1,000Wh buys eight to fourteen hours and 2,000Wh buys a full day.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Measure your own appliance with a plug-in energy meter before you spend anything, keep the door shut when the power goes out, and remember that the goal is cold food rather than a running compressor. Cycling the fridge in bursts is the cheapest capacity upgrade available.
          </p>
